  "text": "```verilog\nmodule areset_deassert_sync #(\n    parameter CHAINS = 2,\n    parameter RST_POL = 1'b1\n)(\n    input  clk,\n    input  async_rst_i,\n    output sync_rst_o\n);\n\n    localparam DEPTH = (CHAINS > 0) ? CHAINS : 1;\n\n    reg [DEPTH-1:0] sync_reg;\n\n    // Compute the active reset value based on polarity\n    // If RST_POL is 1, active reset is 1; if RST_POL is 0, active reset is 0\n    wire active_rst = (RST_POL == 1'b1) ? async_rst_i : ~async_rst_i;\n\n    // Synchronize the active reset signal\n    // The sync chain captures the active reset value\n    // When active_rst is high (active), sync_reg will eventually become all 1s\n    // When active_rst is low (inactive), sync_reg will eventually become all 0s\n\n    // We want sync_rst_o to be the active reset level in the destination domain\n    // So sync_rst_o should be 1 when reset is active, 0 when reset is inactive\n    // This means sync_rst_o = sync_reg[DEPTH-1] (the last stage of the synchronizer)\n\n    // But we need to handle the polarity correctly:\n    // - If RST_POL = 1: active reset is 1, so sync_rst_o should be 1 when reset active\n    // - If RST_POL = 0: active reset is 0, so sync_rst_o should be 0 when reset active\n    //   In this case, we need to invert the synchronized signal\n\n    // Actually, let's think about this more carefully.\n    // The output sync_rst_o should represent the reset signal in the destination domain.\n    // The polarity of the output should match RST_POL.\n    // So if RST_POL = 1, sync_rst_o = 1 means reset is active.\n    // If RST_POL = 0, sync_rst_o = 0 means reset is active.\n\n    // The input async_rst_i has the same polarity as RST_POL.\n    // So async_rst_i = 1 means reset active if RST_POL = 1, and async_rst_i = 0 means reset active if RST_POL = 0.\n\n    // We synchronize async_rst_i directly. The synchronized value will have the same polarity.\n    // So sync_rst_o = sync_reg[DEPTH-1] should work directly.\n\n    // For prompt assertion: when async_rst_i changes to active, we want sync_rst_o to become active quickly.\n    // The standard 2-FF synchronizer takes 2 clock cycles to propagate.\n    // To make assertion prompt, we can use an asynchronous set/reset on the first flip-flop.\n\n    // Let's use a different approach:\n    // - Use an asynchronous reset on the first stage for prompt assertion\n    // - Use the chain for clean deassertion\n\n    // Actually, the standard approach for reset synchronizers is:\n    // - The reset is asserted asynchronously (promptly)\n    // - The reset is deasserted synchronously (cleanly)\n\n    // For RST_POL = 1 (active high):\n    // - When async_rst_i goes high, sync_rst_o should go high promptly\n    // - When async_rst_i goes low, sync_rst_o should go low after CHAINS clock cycles\n\n    // For RST_POL = 0 (active low):\n    // - When async_rst_i goes low, sync_rst_o should go low promptly\n    // - When async_rst_i goes high, sync_rst_o should go high after CHAINS clock cycles\n\n    // Implementation:\n    // We can use a chain of flip-flops where the first one has an asynchronous reset\n    // that is active when the reset is active.\n\n    // For RST_POL = 1:\n    //   async_rst_i = 1 means active. We want sync_rst_o to be 1 promptly.\n    //   We can asynchronously set the first FF to 1 when async_rst_i = 1.\n    //   Then the chain propagates the 1s. When async_rst_i goes to 0, the chain\n    //   will eventually clear to 0s.\n\n    // For RST_POL = 0:\n    //   async_rst_i = 0 means active. We want sync_rst_o to be 0 promptly.\n    //   We can asynchronously clear the first FF to 0 when async_rst_i = 0.\n    //   Then the chain propagates the 0s. When async_rst_i goes to 1, the chain\n    //   will eventually set to 1s.\n\n    // Let's implement this with a generic approach.\n\n    // The key insight: we want the output to follow the input promptly on assertion\n    // and to deassert cleanly over CHAINS cycles.\n\n    // A common technique is to use a \"reset synchronizer\" that:\n    // 1. Has an asynchronous path for assertion\n    // 2. Has a synchronous path for deassertion\n\n    // For RST_POL = 1:\n    //   sync_reg[0] is asynchronously set to 1 when async_rst_i = 1\n    //   sync_reg[0] is synchronously cleared to 0 when async_rst_i = 0 (on clk edge)\n    //   sync_reg[i] = sync_reg[i-1] for i > 0\n    //   sync_rst_o = sync_reg[DEPTH-1]\n\n    // For RST_POL = 0:\n    //   sync_reg[0] is asynchronously cleared to 0 when async_rst_i = 0\n    //   sync_reg[0] is synchronously set to 1 when async_rst_i = 1 (on clk edge)\n    //   sync_reg[i] = sync_reg[i-1] for i > 0\n    //   sync_rst_o = sync_reg[DEPTH-1]\n\n    // Let's write this out.\n\n    // For RST_POL = 1:\n    //   async set: async_rst_i\n    //   sync clear: ~async_rst_i (i.e., when async_rst_i is 0)\n    //   Initial value: 1 (reset active by default)\n\n    // For RST_POL = 0:\n    //   async clear: ~async_rst_i (i.e., when async_rst_i is 0)\n    //   sync set: async_rst_i (i.e., when async_rst_i is 1)\n    //   Initial value: 0 (reset active by default)\n\n    // We can unify this by noting:\n    //   For RST_POL = 1: async_set = async_rst_i, sync_clr = ~async_rst_i, init = 1\n    //   For RST_POL = 0: async_clr = ~async_rst_i, sync_set = async_rst_i, init = 0\n\n    // Let's use a case statement or conditional logic.\n\n    // Actually, let's just write two separate always blocks based on RST_POL.\n\n    // But since RST_POL is a parameter, we can use generate or just write it conditionally.\n\n    // Let's use a simpler approach: we'll create the synchronizer chain where\n    // the first stage has the appropriate async behavior.\n\n    // For RST_POL = 1:\n    always @(posedge clk or posedge async_rst_i) begin\n        if (async_rst_i)\n            sync_reg[0] <= 1'b1;\n        else\n            sync_reg[0] <= 1'b0;\n    end\n\n    // For RST_POL = 0:\n    // always @(posedge clk or negedge async_rst_i) begin\n    //     if (!async_rst_i)\n    //         sync_reg[0] <= 1'b0;\n    //     else\n    //         sync_reg[0] <= 1'b1;\n    // end\n\n    // We need to handle both cases. Let's use a generate block or just write it with\n    // the parameter.\n\n    // Since we can't easily switch between posedge and negedge in a single always block\n    // based on a parameter in Verilog-2001, let's use two separate always blocks\n    // and use a wire to select the output.\n\n    // Actually, we can write it more cleanly. Let's define:\n    //   async_active = (RST_POL == 1) ? async_rst_i : ~async_rst_i\n    //   sync_active  = (RST_POL == 1) ? 1'b1 : 1'b0  // value when active\n    //   sync_inactive = (RST_POL == 1) ? 1'b0 : 1'b1  // value when inactive\n\n    // But the async edge direction depends on RST_POL.\n\n    // Let's just write two separate implementations and select based on RST_POL.\n\n    // For RST_POL = 1:\n    wire [DEPTH-1:0] sync_reg_pos;\n    // For RST_POL = 0:\n    wire [DEPTH-1:0] sync_reg_neg;\n\n    // RST_POL = 1 implementation\n    generate\n        if (RST_POL == 1'b1) begin : gen_pos\n            always @(posedge clk or posedge async_rst_i) begin\n                if (async_rst_i)\n                    sync_reg_pos[0] <= 1'b1;\n                else\n                    sync_reg_pos[0] <= 1'b0;\n            end\n            genvar i;\n            for (i = 1; i < DEPTH; i = i + 1) begin : gen_chain_pos\n                always @(posedge clk) begin\n                    sync_reg_pos[i] <= sync_reg_pos[i-1];\n                end\n            end\n        end else begin : gen_neg\n            always @(posedge clk or negedge async_rst_i) begin\n                if (!async_rst_i)\n                    sync_reg_neg[0] <= 1'b0;\n                else\n                    sync_reg_neg[0] <= 1'b1;\n            end\n            genvar i;\n            for (i = 1; i < DEPTH; i = i + 1) begin : gen_chain_neg\n                always @(posedge clk) begin\n                    sync_reg_neg[i] <= sync_reg_neg[i-1];\n                end\n            end\n        end\n    endgenerate\n\n    assign sync_rst_o = (RST_POL == 1'b1) ? sync_reg_pos[DEPTH-1] : sync_reg_neg[DEPTH-1];\n\nendmodule\n```<|im_end|>",
